course: dictionary
lesson: 14759
title: Big words for big ideas
level: primary-5
stars: 2
explain: To propound something is to put an idea, a theory or a question forward so that people can think about it. It is a formal word, so you will meet it in books, in science and in exams.
explain: Read the clues around it: «The scientist propounded a new theory about how plants grow.» A scientist puts a theory forward, and then other people think about it and test it. So propound means to put forward for thinking.
explain: The words around propound often tell you what is being put forward. «The philosopher propounded a question that puzzled the whole class.» The question is what the philosopher put forward, so propound fits.
fact: Propound is a verb: I propound, he propounds, she propounded, they are propounding.
fact: «Propound» means to put forward an idea, theory or question for people to think about.
fact: Propound is a formal word, so it is used more in writing, science and school exams than in everyday chat.
fact: You can propound a theory, an idea or a question.
fact: A good check is to swap in "put forward" and see if the sentence still makes sense.
exercise: «The scientist propounded a new theory about the stars.» What does propounded mean? | Wrapped up tightly | Hid from view | Forgot completely | *Put forward an idea for people to think about
exercise: «The philosopher propounded a question that puzzled the whole class.» Which word is closest in meaning to propounded? | Ate | Forgot | Dropped | *Put forward
exercise: Which sentence uses propound correctly? | *Ben propounded a riddle for the class to solve. | Ben propounded a loud sneeze. | Ben propounded his shoes under the bed. | Ben propounded a sandwich for lunch.
test: «Scientists propound theories so that others can test them.» What does propound mean? | *Put forward for people to think about | Stop people from thinking | Sleep soundly | Buy in a shop
test: Which of these could you propound? | *A question for the class to think about | A wet umbrella | A bag of crisps | A sleepy cat
exam: Mrs Hale says, «The historian will propound a new view of the war.» What does propound mean here? | *To put forward a view for people to think about | To break into small pieces | To forget a name | To finish a task quickly
